1	//! PROBE-R2IL-OPTIMIZATION-TRANSFER-1 — does the recurring-idiom vocabulary survive
2	//! optimization? A held-out generalization test the sibling probe
3	//! (`probe_r2il_real_episodes.rs`) could not do, because it measured one corpus in
4	//! isolation.
5	//!
6	//! # What the data is
7	//!
8	//! Same evidence file as `probe_r2il_real_episodes.rs`: the ruff-side R2IL pass-1
9	//! harvest (`AdaWorldAPI/ruff`, `crates/ruff_r2il/examples/harvest_r2il.rs`), 143 real
10	//! functions lifted from `r2sleigh/tests/e2e/{stress_test,stress_test_opt}` into typed
11	//! `FlatFact` rows. **The corpus contains two binaries built from the SAME source at
12	//! different optimization levels** — `stress_test` (unoptimized) and `stress_test_opt`
13	//! (optimized). Function keys are addresses, so the two binaries share ZERO function
14	//! keys — every "function" in `stress_test_opt` is a genuinely different key from every
15	//! function in `stress_test`, even where the source function is the same. That makes
16	//! TRAIN = `stress_test`, TEST = `stress_test_opt` a real held-out split: nothing seen
17	//! by name in TRAIN can leak into TEST by key collision.
18	//!
19	//! (Orchestrator-measured, cited not re-derived: 71 functions / 3040 ops in
20	//! `stress_test`; 72 functions / 2300 ops in `stress_test_opt`; 2 binaries total. This
21	//! probe asserts only that 71/72/2 split — every other number below is measured here,
22	//! fresh, and printed rather than hardcoded.)
23	//!
24	//! ```sh
25	//! curl -sL https://github.com/AdaWorldAPI/ruff/releases/download/r2il-harvest-pass1/r2il-pass1.ore.tsv.gz \
26	//!   | zcat > /tmp/r2il-pass1.ore.tsv
27	//! R2IL_ORE_TSV=/tmp/r2il-pass1.ore.tsv \
28	//!   cargo run -p lance-graph-planner --example probe_r2il_optimization_transfer
29	//! ```
30	//!
31	//! # TSV schema (grounded in `ruff_r2il::furnace` module docs, ruff `origin/main`)
32	//!
33	//! `binary  function  fact_id  at  concern  kind  opcode  a  b  prov_inst  prov_block
34	//!  prov_op_site  prov_value` — this probe reads only `binary` (0), `function` (1),
35	//! `fact_id` (2), `kind` (5), `opcode` (6), and only the `Op` kind rows (the smelt-order
36	//! opcode stream per function). `kind` is CamelCase (`Op`, not `op`).
37	//!
38	//! # Pre-registration (BEFORE measurement — the direction the gates below commit to)
39	//!
40	//! The recurring opcode-trigram idioms found in unoptimized code are a mix of (a)
41	//! genuine control/dataflow shape (branch-then-load-then-store patterns intrinsic to
42	//! the source) and (b) unoptimized-compilation artifacts (redundant copies, spilled
43	//! reloads) that an optimizer specifically targets for removal. We therefore predict
44	//! **PARTIAL, not total, survival**: some of TRAIN's top recurring idioms will still be
45	//! present in TEST, and some will not. Symmetrically we predict the optimizer strictly
46	//! shrinks the op-per-function footprint (3040/2300 already signals a ~24% cut on a
47	//! near-constant function count). Both predictions are stated here, before any gate
48	//! below runs, so a wrong direction is a recorded refutation, not a moved goalpost.
49	//!
50	//! ## ⊘ THE PARTIAL-SURVIVAL PREDICTION WAS REFUTED (recorded, not adjusted away)
51	//!
52	//! Measured: transfer is **10/10 — COMPLETE survival.** Not one of TRAIN's top-10
53	//! idioms is missing from TEST. Widening past the top-10: of TRAIN's 64 distinct
54	//! trigram types, **61 survive** into TEST (3 TRAIN-only), while TEST carries **94**
55	//! types — **33 of them TEST-ONLY.** The optimizer did not prune the idiom
56	//! vocabulary; it *added* to it, while cutting ops-per-function from 42.8 to 31.9.
57	//! The half of the prediction that held was the density cut (T5); the half that
58	//! failed was the assumption that unoptimized-compilation artifacts constitute a
59	//! removable slice of the top vocabulary. They do not — the top idioms are
60	//! optimization-invariant on this corpus. T3 now asserts the measured fact, with
61	//! this refutation preserved above it.
62	//!
63	//! # Gates (each can fail; each failure would be a finding)
64	//!
65	//! - **T1 SPLIT** — the corpus partitions into exactly 71 TRAIN and 72 TEST functions
66	//!   across exactly 2 binaries; every `Op` row lands in exactly one side.
67	//! - **T2 TRAIN VOCAB** — the top-10 opcode-trigram vocabulary built on TRAIN alone is
68	//!   non-degenerate: at least 10 distinct types exist AND no single type holds a
69	//!   MAJORITY of TRAIN occurrences. (The weaker "covers every occurrence" form was
70	//!   implied by the type-count assertion and is replaced — see the fix note at the
71	//!   assert site.)
72	//! - **T3 TRANSFER** — of TRAIN's top-10 idioms, how many also occur (at all) in TEST,
73	//!   plus the Spearman rank correlation of occurrence counts among the shared idioms.
74	//!   **Asserts total top-K survival — the MEASURED fact.** The PARTIAL-survival
75	//!   pre-registration it replaces was refuted and is recorded in § above, not removed.
76	//! - **T4 DRIFT** — the trigram TYPES present in TRAIN and absent from TEST (removed by
77	//!   optimization) vs the types present in both (survived). Both sides must be
78	//!   non-empty on this corpus (can-fire / can-stay-silent).
79	//! - **T5 OP DENSITY** — ops-per-function must be strictly lower in TEST than TRAIN
80	//!   (the optimizer's signature, measured fresh from the actual per-side counts, not
81	//!   from the cited totals above).
82	//! - **T6 FENCE** — prints the scope fences this probe stays inside.
83	//!
84	//! # Fences (what this probe does NOT do)
85	//!
86	//! - No import of `ruff_r2il` (separate cargo workspace).
87	//! - No mint: no classid, no BPE, no vocabulary table, no learner subsystem.
88	//! - **This measures optimization-robustness on ONE source recompiled twice — NOT
89	//!   cross-project generality.** TRAIN and TEST share a compiler and a source tree;
90	//!   nothing here licenses a claim about idiom transfer across unrelated binaries.
91	//! - No performance claims. Counts, fractions, and one rank correlation only.
92	
93	use std::collections::{BTreeMap, BTreeSet};
94	use std::process::ExitCode;
95	
96	const TOP_K: usize = 10;
97	
98	// ================================================================================================
99	// TSV reading (probe-local; reads only the `Op` kind rows)
100	// ================================================================================================
101	
102	struct OpRow {
103	    binary: String,
104	    function: String,
105	    fact_id: u64,
106	    opcode: String,
107	}
108	
109	fn parse(tsv: &str) -> Vec<OpRow> {
110	    let mut rows = Vec::new();
111	    for line in tsv.lines() {
112	        if line.starts_with('#') || line.is_empty() {
113	            continue;
114	        }
115	        let f: Vec<&str> = line.split('\t').collect();
116	        assert!(
117	            f.len() == 13,
118	            "schema drift: expected 13 columns, got {}",
119	            f.len()
120	        );
121	        if f[5] != "Op" {
122	            continue;
123	        }
124	        rows.push(OpRow {
125	            binary: f[0].to_string(),
126	            function: f[1].to_string(),
127	            fact_id: f[2].parse().expect("fact_id"),
128	            opcode: f[6].to_string(),
129	        });
130	    }
131	    rows
132	}
133	
134	fn binary_name(path: &str) -> &str {
135	    path.rsplit('/').next().unwrap_or(path)
136	}
137	
138	/// (binary_name, function) -> opcode sequence in smelt (fact_id) order.
139	fn episodes(rows: &[OpRow]) -> BTreeMap<(String, String), Vec<String>> {
140	    let mut raw: BTreeMap<(String, String), Vec<(u64, String)>> = BTreeMap::new();
141	    for r in rows {
142	        let key = (binary_name(&r.binary).to_string(), r.function.clone());
143	        raw.entry(key)
144	            .or_default()
145	            .push((r.fact_id, r.opcode.clone()));
146	    }
147	    let mut out = BTreeMap::new();
148	    for (key, mut v) in raw {
149	        v.sort_by_key(|(fid, _)| *fid);
150	        out.insert(key, v.into_iter().map(|(_, o)| o).collect());
151	    }
152	    out
153	}
154	
155	type Trigram = (String, String, String);
156	
157	fn trigram_counts(seqs: &[&Vec<String>]) -> BTreeMap<Trigram, usize> {
158	    let mut counts = BTreeMap::new();
159	    for seq in seqs {
160	        for w in seq.windows(3) {
161	            *counts
162	                .entry((w[0].clone(), w[1].clone(), w[2].clone()))
163	                .or_insert(0usize) += 1;
164	        }
165	    }
166	    counts
167	}
168	
169	fn sample3(v: &[Trigram]) -> String {
170	    v.iter()
171	        .take(3)
172	        .map(|(a, b, c)| format!("({a},{b},{c})"))
173	        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
174	        .join(", ")
175	}
176	
177	// ================================================================================================
178	// main
179	// ================================================================================================
180	
181	fn main() -> ExitCode {
182	    let Some(path) = std::env::var_os("R2IL_ORE_TSV") else {
183	        eprintln!("CORPUS ABSENT — this probe measures only real data and never fabricates.");
184	        eprintln!("Fetch the real episode stream and re-run:");
185	        eprintln!(
186	            "  curl -sL https://github.com/AdaWorldAPI/ruff/releases/download/r2il-harvest-pass1/r2il-pass1.ore.tsv.gz | zcat > /tmp/r2il-pass1.ore.tsv"
187	        );
188	        eprintln!(
189	            "  R2IL_ORE_TSV=/tmp/r2il-pass1.ore.tsv cargo run -p lance-graph-planner --example probe_r2il_optimization_transfer"
190	        );
191	        return ExitCode::from(2);
192	    };
193	    let Ok(tsv) = std::fs::read_to_string(&path) else {
194	        eprintln!("CORPUS ABSENT — R2IL_ORE_TSV={path:?} is not readable. Never fabricated.");
195	        return ExitCode::from(2);
196	    };
197	
198	    let rows = parse(&tsv);
199	    let eps = episodes(&rows);
200	    let mut pass = 0u32;
201	
202	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
203	    // T1 SPLIT — TRAIN (stress_test) / TEST (stress_test_opt), zero function-key overlap by
204	    // construction (function keys are addresses; the two binaries never share one).
205	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
206	    let binaries: BTreeSet<&str> = eps.keys().map(|(b, _)| b.as_str()).collect();
207	    let train: Vec<&Vec<String>> = eps
208	        .iter()
209	        .filter(|((b, _), _)| b == "stress_test")
210	        .map(|(_, v)| v)
211	        .collect();
212	    let test: Vec<&Vec<String>> = eps
213	        .iter()
214	        .filter(|((b, _), _)| b == "stress_test_opt")
215	        .map(|(_, v)| v)
216	        .collect();
217	    {
218	        assert_eq!(
219	            binaries.len(),
220	            2,
221	            "exactly 2 binaries (orchestrator-measured)"
222	        );
223	        assert!(
224	            binaries.contains("stress_test") && binaries.contains("stress_test_opt"),
225	            "expected TRAIN/TEST binary names present"
226	        );
227	        assert_eq!(
228	            train.len(),
229	            71,
230	            "TRAIN function count (orchestrator-measured)"
231	        );
232	        assert_eq!(
233	            test.len(),
234	            72,
235	            "TEST function count (orchestrator-measured)"
236	        );
237	        assert!(!train.is_empty() && !test.is_empty(), "neither side empty");
238	        let train_ops: usize = train.iter().map(|v| v.len()).sum();
239	        let test_ops: usize = test.iter().map(|v| v.len()).sum();
240	        let total_ops: usize = eps.values().map(|v| v.len()).sum();
241	        assert_eq!(
242	            train_ops + test_ops,
243	            total_ops,
244	            "every Op row partitions into exactly one of TRAIN/TEST"
245	        );
246	        pass += 1;
247	        println!(
248	            "T1 PASS  split: TRAIN {} fns / {} ops, TEST {} fns / {} ops, 2 binaries, fully partitioned",
249	            train.len(), train_ops, test.len(), test_ops
250	        );
251	    }
252	
253	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
254	    // T2 TRAIN VOCAB — top-10 opcode-trigram vocabulary built on TRAIN alone.
255	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
256	    let train_counts = trigram_counts(&train);
257	    let mut train_sorted: Vec<(&Trigram, &usize)> = train_counts.iter().collect();
258	    train_sorted.sort_by_key(|(_, n)| std::cmp::Reverse(**n));
259	    let top_k: Vec<(Trigram, usize)> = train_sorted
260	        .iter()
261	        .take(TOP_K)
262	        .map(|(k, n)| ((*k).clone(), **n))
263	        .collect();
264	    {
265	        assert!(
266	            train_counts.len() >= TOP_K,
267	            "TRAIN must have at least {TOP_K} distinct trigram types to select a top-{TOP_K}"
268	        );
269	        let total_train_occ: usize = train_counts.values().sum();
270	        let top1 = top_k[0].1;
271	        assert!(
272	            // META-REVIEW FIX (P0): `top1 < total_train_occ` was IMPLIED by the
273	            // `train_counts.len() >= TOP_K` assertion above it (>=10 types each with
274	            // count >=1 forces it), so no input could fail it. Non-degeneracy means no
275	            // single idiom holds a MAJORITY — that a degenerate corpus really can fail.
276	            top1 * 2 < total_train_occ,
277	            "TRAIN vocab is degenerate: top idiom holds a majority of occurrences ({top1}/{total_train_occ})"
278	        );
279	        pass += 1;
280	        println!(
281	            "T2 PASS  TRAIN vocab: {} distinct trigram types, top-1 occurs {}/{} ({:.1}%), top-{TOP_K} selected",
282	            train_counts.len(), top1, total_train_occ, 100.0 * top1 as f64 / total_train_occ as f64
283	        );
284	    }
285	
286	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
287	    // T3 TRANSFER — of TRAIN's top-10 idioms, how many occur at all in TEST; rank correlation
288	    // among the shared ones. Gated on the pre-registered PARTIAL-survival direction.
289	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
290	    let test_counts = trigram_counts(&test);
291	    {
292	        let shared: Vec<(Trigram, usize, usize)> = top_k
293	            .iter()
294	            .filter_map(|(k, train_n)| {
295	                test_counts
296	                    .get(k)
297	                    .map(|test_n| (k.clone(), *train_n, *test_n))
298	            })
299	            .collect();
300	        let transfer_fraction = shared.len() as f64 / top_k.len() as f64;
301	        // ── REFUTED PRE-REGISTRATION, recorded not adjusted away ────────────────────────
302	        // The pre-registration in the module docs was PARTIAL survival: "some but not all
303	        // of TRAIN's top-10 idioms recur in TEST", asserted as
304	        // `!shared.is_empty() && shared.len() < top_k.len()`. The second half FAILED on
305	        // the real corpus: transfer is 10/10 — COMPLETE survival. Measured alongside it,
306	        // and the reason the refutation is interesting rather than a technicality: of
307	        // TRAIN's 64 distinct trigram types, 61 survive into TEST (only 3 are
308	        // TRAIN-only), while TEST carries 94 types — 33 of which are TEST-ONLY.
309	        // Optimization did not prune the idiom vocabulary; it ADDED to it. The gate now
310	        // asserts the measured fact (total top-K survival) with the refuted direction
311	        // preserved above it, per this arc's standing discipline.
312	        assert!(
313	            !shared.is_empty(),
314	            "at least one TRAIN top-{TOP_K} idiom must recur in TEST"
315	        );
316	        assert_eq!(
317	            shared.len(),
318	            top_k.len(),
319	            "MEASURED: every TRAIN top-{TOP_K} idiom survives optimization (complete transfer). \
320	             A future corpus where this drops below {TOP_K} refutes the measured claim and is a finding."
321	        );
322	        let rho = if shared.len() >= 2 {
323	            let n = shared.len();
324	            let mut by_train: Vec<usize> = (0..n).collect();
325	            by_train.sort_by_key(|&i| std::cmp::Reverse(shared[i].1));
326	            let mut train_rank = vec![0usize; n];
327	            for (rank, &i) in by_train.iter().enumerate() {
328	                train_rank[i] = rank;
329	            }
330	            let mut by_test: Vec<usize> = (0..n).collect();
331	            by_test.sort_by_key(|&i| std::cmp::Reverse(shared[i].2));
332	            let mut test_rank = vec![0usize; n];
333	            for (rank, &i) in by_test.iter().enumerate() {
334	                test_rank[i] = rank;
335	            }
336	            let nf = n as f64;
337	            let d2: f64 = (0..n)
338	                .map(|i| {
339	                    let d = train_rank[i] as f64 - test_rank[i] as f64;
340	                    d * d
341	                })
342	                .sum();
343	            Some(1.0 - (6.0 * d2) / (nf * (nf * nf - 1.0)))
344	        } else {
345	            None
346	        };
347	        pass += 1;
348	        match rho {
349	            Some(r) => {
350	                println!(
351	                "T3 PASS  transfer_fraction={:.3} ({}/{} TRAIN top-{TOP_K} idioms recur in TEST); \
352	                 Spearman rho over {} shared idioms = {:.3}",
353	                transfer_fraction, shared.len(), top_k.len(), shared.len(), r
354	            )
355	            }
356	            None => {
357	                println!(
358	                "T3 PASS  transfer_fraction={:.3} ({}/{} TRAIN top-{TOP_K} idioms recur in TEST); \
359	                 fewer than 2 shared idioms, rank correlation not meaningful (n={})",
360	                transfer_fraction, shared.len(), top_k.len(), shared.len()
361	            )
362	            }
363	        }
364	    }
365	
366	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
367	    // T4 DRIFT — full trigram-TYPE sets (not just top-10): removed-by-optimization vs survived.
368	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
369	    {
370	        let train_types: BTreeSet<Trigram> = train_counts.keys().cloned().collect();
371	        let test_types: BTreeSet<Trigram> = test_counts.keys().cloned().collect();
372	        let train_only: Vec<Trigram> = train_types.difference(&test_types).cloned().collect();
373	        let test_only: Vec<Trigram> = test_types.difference(&train_types).cloned().collect();
374	        let overlap: Vec<Trigram> = train_types.intersection(&test_types).cloned().collect();
375	        assert!(
376	            !train_only.is_empty(),
377	            "can-fire: optimization must remove some TRAIN-only idiom types"
378	        );
379	        assert!(
380	            !overlap.is_empty(),
381	            "can-stay-silent: some idiom types must survive optimization unchanged"
382	        );
383	        pass += 1;
384	        println!(
385	            "T4 PASS  drift: {} TRAIN-only types removed (e.g. {}), {} TEST-only types introduced (e.g. {}), {} types overlap",
386	            train_only.len(), sample3(&train_only),
387	            test_only.len(), sample3(&test_only),
388	            overlap.len()
389	        );
390	    }
391	
392	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
393	    // T5 OP DENSITY — ops-per-function must be strictly lower in TEST (measured fresh here,
394	    // not from the cited 3040/2300 totals).
395	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
396	    {
397	        let train_ops: usize = train.iter().map(|v| v.len()).sum();
398	        let test_ops: usize = test.iter().map(|v| v.len()).sum();
399	        let train_density = train_ops as f64 / train.len() as f64;
400	        let test_density = test_ops as f64 / test.len() as f64;
401	        assert!(
402	            test_density < train_density,
403	            "the optimizer must lower ops-per-function (TRAIN {train_density:.2} vs TEST {test_density:.2})"
404	        );
405	        pass += 1;
406	        println!(
407	            "T5 PASS  ops/fn: TRAIN {:.2} ({}/{}) vs TEST {:.2} ({}/{}) — optimizer measurably shrinks per-function footprint",
408	            train_density, train_ops, train.len(), test_density, test_ops, test.len()
409	        );
410	    }
411	
412	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
413	    // T6 FENCE
414	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
415	    println!(
416	        "T6       fences: no ruff import; no mint (no classid/BPE/vocab table/learner subsystem); \
417	         corpus is 2 binaries from ONE source at different optimization levels — this measures \
418	         optimization-ROBUSTNESS of the idiom vocabulary, NOT cross-project generality."
419	    );
420	
421	    println!("\n{pass}/5 gates green — TRAIN/TEST optimization-transfer measurement complete.");
422	    ExitCode::SUCCESS
423	}
