1	//! PROBE-R2IL-DEFUSE-MACROS-1 — testing the PRESCRIPTION from
2	//! `probe_r2il_real_episodes`'s E5 headline, not just citing it.
3	//!
4	//! # The finding under test
5	//!
6	//! `probe_r2il_real_episodes` (this crate, same directory) measured, on this same
7	//! real corpus, that of 380 occurrences of the top recurring opcode trigram
8	//! `(int_add, copy, store)` only 1 was dataflow-chained, against a 31.5% base rate
9	//! of adjacent-op def-use linkage (those numbers are ORCHESTRATOR-MEASURED — cited
10	//! here, never re-asserted as a constant by this probe). Its conclusion: "sequential
11	//! adjacency is not composition — the macro carrier must be the DEF-USE CHAIN, never
12	//! the linear opcode window."
13	//!
14	//! This probe stops using the linear window entirely and builds macros from DEF-USE
15	//! CHAINS instead, then measures whether the chain carrier is actually better — i.e.
16	//! whether the prescription holds, or whether the chain vocabulary turns out to be
17	//! sparse/degenerate. Every gate below is pre-registered with a direction and is
18	//! allowed to fail; a failure IS the finding, not a bug in this probe.
19	//!
20	//! # Definition — DEF-USE CHAIN (the whole idea)
21	//!
22	//! Within one episode, a def-use edge exists from op X to op Y when some ValueId in
23	//! X's `OperandOut` set appears in Y's `OperandIn` set, AND X's position precedes
24	//! Y's position in the episode's smelt (fact_id) order. A length-3 CHAIN is X→Y→Z
25	//! under two such edges (X→Y, Y→Z) — **regardless of whether X, Y, Z are adjacent in
26	//! fact_id order.** X<Y<Z in position is a consequence of chaining two forward edges,
27	//! not an extra check. The chain's signature is `(opcode(X), opcode(Y), opcode(Z))`.
28	//!
29	//! Backward uses (an op consuming a value defined by a LATER op — loop-carried
30	//! values across a back-edge) are excluded from edge construction: this pass-1 chain
31	//! is forward-only, straight-line def-use, not full program dataflow (see C6).
32	//!
33	//! # Data contract (grounded in `probe_r2il_real_episodes`'s module docs; not
34	//! re-derived)
35	//!
36	//! env var `R2IL_ORE_TSV` → TSV, `#`-comments, 13 tab-separated columns:
37	//! `binary function fact_id at concern kind opcode a b prov_inst prov_block
38	//! prov_op_site prov_value` (0-indexed: 0=binary, 1=function, 2=fact_id, 5=kind,
39	//! 6=opcode, 8=b, 11=prov_op_site). `kind` is CamelCase (`Op`/`OperandIn`/
40	//! `OperandOut`/`Edge`/`CallSite`). For `OperandIn`/`OperandOut`, `b == ValueId + 1`,
41	//! `b == 0` means no SSA value (skip). An episode = one (binary, function) pair; its
42	//! ops are the `Op` rows in ascending fact_id order, keyed by `prov_op_site`.
43	//! Orchestrator-measured (cited, not asserted by this probe): 143 episodes, 2
44	//! binaries, 5340 Op rows, 7416 OperandIn rows, 4237 OperandOut rows, 0 rows with
45	//! `b == 0` (every operand row carries a real ValueId).
46	//!
47	//! # Pre-registrations — every gate can fail
48	//!
49	//! - **C1 CHAIN EXTRACTION** — chains exist (`total > 0`, can-fire) and recur
50	//!   (`distinct * 10 < total` — margin against the 9^3 pigeonhole bound; a vocabulary this thin
51	//!   would be all-unique and degenerate as a macro carrier).
52	//! - **C2 NOT-THE-WINDOW** — the chain-signature set differs from the adjacent-window
53	//!   opcode-trigram set: symmetric difference non-empty (can-fire, the carriers are
54	//!   NOT the same thing) and intersection non-empty (can-stay-silent, they are not
55	//!   disjoint universes either).
56	//! - **C3 REACHES PAST ADJACENCY** — for the single most-recurring chain signature,
57	//!   some fraction of its occurrences have `z_pos - x_pos > 2`, i.e. skip at least one
58	//!   intervening op (can-fire). The "100% dataflow-chained" fact is true by
59	//!   construction and is explicitly NOT treated as evidence here (falsifiability
60	//!   rule) — only the skip-past-adjacency claim is asserted.
61	//! - **C4 CONCENTRATION** — pre-registered: the def-use chain vocabulary's top-10
62	//!   occupancy share is HIGHER than the linear-window trigram vocabulary's, on the
63	//!   same episodes (the claim: dataflow recurrence is a cleaner macro signal than
64	//!   incidental adjacency). Refuted if the inequality does not hold.
65	//! - **C5 SPAN** — pre-registered: the median chain span (`z_pos - x_pos`) exceeds 2
66	//!   (the pure-adjacency span), i.e. chains genuinely reach past neighboring ops, not
67	//!   merely restate them.
68	//! - **C6 FENCE** — prints the scope fences (recomputed corpus binary count as a live
69	//!   anchor, not a memorized constant) and states explicitly that this is a pass-1,
70	//!   single-episode, seven-opcode chain — not full program dataflow.
71	//!
72	//! # Fences
73	//!
74	//! - No import of `ruff_r2il` (separate cargo workspace) — the TSV schema is cited
75	//!   and re-derived from the file itself, same discipline as `probe_r2il_real_episodes`.
76	//! - No mint: no classid, no BPE, no vocabulary table, no learner subsystem.
77	//! - Not full program dataflow: no alias analysis, no memory-SSA, no interprocedural
78	//!   edges, no loop-carried (backward) def-use — forward, intra-episode, pass-1 only.
79	//! - No performance claims. Counts, rates, and one NARS-style confidence readout
80	//!   (reusing the shipped `revise`/`Stamp` mechanism, nothing new) only.
81	
82	use std::collections::{BTreeMap, HashMap, HashSet};
83	use std::process::ExitCode;
84	
85	use lance_graph_planner::nars::belief::Stamp;
86	use lance_graph_planner::nars::truth::TruthValue;
87	
88	// ================================================================================================
89	// TSV reading (duplicated from `probe_r2il_real_episodes` — separate example binary,
90	// same schema; no cross-example import exists for cargo `--example` targets)
91	// ================================================================================================
92	
93	struct Row {
94	    binary: String,
95	    function: String,
96	    fact_id: u64,
97	    kind: String,
98	    opcode: String,
99	    b: u64,
100	    op_site: String,
101	}
102	
103	fn parse(tsv: &str) -> Vec<Row> {
104	    let mut rows = Vec::new();
105	    for line in tsv.lines() {
106	        if line.starts_with('#') || line.is_empty() {
107	            continue;
108	        }
109	        let f: Vec<&str> = line.split('\t').collect();
110	        assert!(
111	            f.len() == 13,
112	            "schema drift: expected 13 columns, got {}",
113	            f.len()
114	        );
115	        rows.push(Row {
116	            binary: f[0].to_string(),
117	            function: f[1].to_string(),
118	            fact_id: f[2].parse().expect("fact_id"),
119	            kind: f[5].to_string(),
120	            opcode: f[6].to_string(),
121	            b: f[8].parse().expect("b"),
122	            op_site: f[11].to_string(),
123	        });
124	    }
125	    rows
126	}
127	
128	/// One real function-episode: the smelt-ordered op sequence plus per-op-site dataflow.
129	struct Episode {
130	    ops: Vec<(String, String)>,       // (op_site, opcode), sorted by fact_id
131	    outs: BTreeMap<String, Vec<u64>>, // op_site -> ValueIds DEFINED
132	    ins: BTreeMap<String, Vec<u64>>,  // op_site -> ValueIds CONSUMED
133	}
134	
135	fn episodes(rows: &[Row]) -> BTreeMap<(String, String), Episode> {
136	    let mut map: BTreeMap<(String, String), Episode> = BTreeMap::new();
137	    let mut op_order: BTreeMap<(String, String), Vec<(u64, String, String)>> = BTreeMap::new();
138	    for r in rows {
139	        let key = (r.binary.clone(), r.function.clone());
140	        let ep = map.entry(key.clone()).or_insert_with(|| Episode {
141	            ops: Vec::new(),
142	            outs: BTreeMap::new(),
143	            ins: BTreeMap::new(),
144	        });
145	        match r.kind.as_str() {
146	            "Op" => op_order.entry(key).or_default().push((
147	                r.fact_id,
148	                r.op_site.clone(),
149	                r.opcode.clone(),
150	            )),
151	            "OperandOut" if r.b != 0 => {
152	                ep.outs.entry(r.op_site.clone()).or_default().push(r.b - 1);
153	            }
154	            "OperandIn" if r.b != 0 => {
155	                ep.ins.entry(r.op_site.clone()).or_default().push(r.b - 1);
156	            }
157	            _ => {}
158	        }
159	    }
160	    for (key, mut ops) in op_order {
161	        ops.sort_by_key(|(fid, _, _)| *fid);
162	        map.get_mut(&key)
163	            .expect("episode exists for every op row")
164	            .ops = ops.into_iter().map(|(_, site, opc)| (site, opc)).collect();
165	    }
166	    map
167	}
168	
169	// ================================================================================================
170	// Def-use chain extraction — the new carrier under test
171	// ================================================================================================
172	
173	struct ChainOcc {
174	    sig: (String, String, String),
175	    x_pos: usize,
176	    y_pos: usize,
177	    z_pos: usize,
178	    ep_key: (String, String),
179	}
180	
181	/// Extract every length-3 forward def-use chain, per episode, independent of
182	/// linear/fact_id adjacency. Backward (loop-carried) uses are excluded by
183	/// construction (edge requires `pos(use) > pos(def)`).
184	fn extract_chains(eps: &BTreeMap<(String, String), Episode>) -> Vec<ChainOcc> {
185	    let mut all = Vec::new();
186	    for (key, ep) in eps.iter() {
187	        let mut pos: HashMap<&str, usize> = HashMap::new();
188	        let mut opcode_of: HashMap<&str, &str> = HashMap::new();
189	        for (i, (site, opc)) in ep.ops.iter().enumerate() {
190	            pos.insert(site.as_str(), i);
191	            opcode_of.insert(site.as_str(), opc.as_str());
192	        }
193	        // META-REVIEW FIX (P1): the whole chain extraction assumes `prov_op_site` is
194	        // UNIQUE within an episode — `pos.insert` silently overwrites otherwise, and
195	        // every span/skip figure below would be computed against a wrong position map
196	        // while every gate still passed. Make the assumption a gate that can fire.
197	        assert_eq!(
198	            pos.len(),
199	            ep.ops.len(),
200	            "prov_op_site must be unique within an episode"
201	        );
202	
203	        // uses_of: ValueId -> [(pos, op_site)] consuming it
204	        let mut uses_of: HashMap<u64, Vec<(usize, &str)>> = HashMap::new();
205	        for (site, vals) in &ep.ins {
206	            let Some(&p) = pos.get(site.as_str()) else {
207	                continue; // defensive: an operand row for an op not in the Op set
208	            };
209	            for v in vals {
210	                uses_of.entry(*v).or_default().push((p, site.as_str()));
211	            }
212	        }
213	
214	        // def_of: ValueId -> (pos, op_site) — earliest position defensively, in case
215	        // of an anomalous multi-def value; SSA on this corpus should give one def.
216	        let mut def_of: HashMap<u64, (usize, &str)> = HashMap::new();
217	        for (site, vals) in &ep.outs {
218	            let Some(&p) = pos.get(site.as_str()) else {
219	                continue;
220	            };
221	            for v in vals {
222	                def_of
223	                    .entry(*v)
224	                    .and_modify(|e| {
225	                        if p < e.0 {
226	                            *e = (p, site.as_str());
227	                        }
228	                    })
229	                    .or_insert((p, site.as_str()));
230	            }
231	        }
232	
233	        // forward_edges: X -> {Y} where a value defined at X (pos px) is consumed at
234	        // Y (pos py), py > px. FORWARD ONLY — this is the back-edge exclusion fence.
235	        let mut fwd: HashMap<&str, HashSet<&str>> = HashMap::new();
236	        for (v, &(px, x_site)) in &def_of {
237	            if let Some(users) = uses_of.get(v) {
238	                for &(py, y_site) in users {
239	                    if py > px {
240	                        fwd.entry(x_site).or_default().insert(y_site);
241	                    }
242	                }
243	            }
244	        }
245	
246	        // Chains X -> Y -> Z: chaining two forward edges guarantees x_pos < y_pos <
247	        // z_pos, so no extra distinctness/ordering check is needed here.
248	        for (&x_site, ys) in &fwd {
249	            for &y_site in ys.iter() {
250	                let Some(zs) = fwd.get(y_site) else {
251	                    continue;
252	                };
253	                for &z_site in zs.iter() {
254	                    let x_pos = *pos.get(x_site).expect("x_site in pos");
255	                    let y_pos = *pos.get(y_site).expect("y_site in pos");
256	                    let z_pos = *pos.get(z_site).expect("z_site in pos");
257	                    let sig = (
258	                        (*opcode_of.get(x_site).expect("x_site opcode")).to_string(),
259	                        (*opcode_of.get(y_site).expect("y_site opcode")).to_string(),
260	                        (*opcode_of.get(z_site).expect("z_site opcode")).to_string(),
261	                    );
262	                    all.push(ChainOcc {
263	                        sig,
264	                        x_pos,
265	                        y_pos,
266	                        z_pos,
267	                        ep_key: key.clone(),
268	                    });
269	                }
270	            }
271	        }
272	    }
273	    all
274	}
275	
276	fn chain_sig_counts(chains: &[ChainOcc]) -> BTreeMap<(String, String, String), usize> {
277	    let mut counts = BTreeMap::new();
278	    for c in chains {
279	        *counts.entry(c.sig.clone()).or_insert(0usize) += 1;
280	    }
281	    counts
282	}
283	
284	/// The carrier this probe REPLACES: adjacent-window opcode trigrams, on the same
285	/// episodes, counted the same way `probe_r2il_real_episodes` counts them.
286	fn window_trigram_counts(
287	    eps: &BTreeMap<(String, String), Episode>,
288	) -> BTreeMap<(String, String, String), usize> {
289	    let mut counts = BTreeMap::new();
290	    for ep in eps.values() {
291	        let opcs: Vec<&str> = ep.ops.iter().map(|(_, o)| o.as_str()).collect();
292	        for w in opcs.windows(3) {
293	            let key = (w[0].to_string(), w[1].to_string(), w[2].to_string());
294	            *counts.entry(key).or_insert(0usize) += 1;
295	        }
296	    }
297	    counts
298	}
299	
300	fn top10_share(counts: &BTreeMap<(String, String, String), usize>) -> f64 {
301	    let mut v: Vec<usize> = counts.values().copied().collect();
302	    v.sort_unstable_by(|a, b| b.cmp(a));
303	    let total: usize = v.iter().sum();
304	    if total == 0 {
305	        return 0.0;
306	    }
307	    v.iter().take(10).sum::<usize>() as f64 / total as f64
308	}
309	
310	fn median_sorted(v: &[usize]) -> f64 {
311	    let n = v.len();
312	    if n % 2 == 1 {
313	        v[n / 2] as f64
314	    } else {
315	        (v[n / 2 - 1] + v[n / 2]) as f64 / 2.0
316	    }
317	}
318	
319	// ================================================================================================
320	// main
321	// ================================================================================================
322	
323	fn main() -> ExitCode {
324	    let Some(path) = std::env::var_os("R2IL_ORE_TSV") else {
325	        eprintln!("CORPUS ABSENT — this probe measures only real data and never fabricates.");
326	        eprintln!("Fetch the real episode stream and re-run:");
327	        eprintln!(
328	            "  curl -sL https://github.com/AdaWorldAPI/ruff/releases/download/r2il-harvest-pass1/r2il-pass1.ore.tsv.gz \\\n    | zcat > /tmp/r2il-pass1.ore.tsv"
329	        );
330	        eprintln!(
331	            "  R2IL_ORE_TSV=/tmp/r2il-pass1.ore.tsv cargo run -p lance-graph-planner --example probe_r2il_defuse_macros"
332	        );
333	        return ExitCode::from(2);
334	    };
335	    let Ok(tsv) = std::fs::read_to_string(&path) else {
336	        eprintln!("CORPUS ABSENT — R2IL_ORE_TSV={path:?} is not readable. Never fabricated.");
337	        return ExitCode::from(2);
338	    };
339	
340	    let rows = parse(&tsv);
341	    let eps = episodes(&rows);
342	    let chains = extract_chains(&eps);
343	    let chain_counts = chain_sig_counts(&chains);
344	    let top_sig = chain_counts
345	        .iter()
346	        .max_by_key(|(_, n)| *n)
347	        .map(|(k, _)| k.clone());
348	    let mut pass = 0u32;
349	
350	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
351	    // C1 CHAIN EXTRACTION — chains exist and recur.
352	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
353	    {
354	        let total = chains.len();
355	        let distinct = chain_counts.len();
356	        assert!(
357	            total > 0,
358	            "C1 can-fire: length-3 def-use chains must exist in this corpus"
359	        );
360	        assert!(
361	            // META-REVIEW FIX (P1): `distinct < total` is unfalsifiable at this corpus
362	            // scale by pigeonhole — 9 opcodes bound the signature space at 9^3 = 729, and
363	            // this corpus yields far more chains than that, so the bare form passes
364	            // necessarily. Assert recurrence WITH MARGIN so the gate can actually fail.
365	            distinct * 10 < total,
366	            "C1 pre-registered: chain signatures recur (distinct {distinct} < total {total}) \
367	             — otherwise the def-use chain vocabulary is degenerate (all-unique, no macro carrier)"
368	        );
369	
370	        // Supplementary confidence readout: how consistently does the single most
371	        // recurring chain signature show up per-episode, using the SHIPPED
372	        // revise+Stamp loop (mirrors `probe_r2il_real_episodes`'s E4 mechanism
373	        // exactly — nothing new minted here).
374	        let mut e_value = f32::NAN;
375	        let mut revised = 0usize;
376	        let mut dropped = 0usize;
377	        if let Some(sig) = &top_sig {
378	            let episodes_with_sig: HashSet<&(String, String)> = chains
379	                .iter()
380	                .filter(|c| &c.sig == sig)
381	                .map(|c| &c.ep_key)
382	                .collect();
383	            let mut truth = TruthValue::new(0.5, 0.05);
384	            let mut stamp = Stamp(0);
385	            for (idx, key) in eps.keys().enumerate() {
386	                if !episodes_with_sig.contains(key) {
387	                    continue;
388	                }
389	                let ev = Stamp::source(idx as u32);
390	                if stamp.disjoint(ev) {
391	                    truth = truth.revise(&TruthValue::new(1.0, 0.9));
392	                    stamp = stamp.union(ev);
393	                    revised += 1;
394	                } else {
395	                    dropped += 1; // CHOICE: mod-64 stamp collision, same mechanism as E4
396	                }
397	            }
398	            e_value = truth.expectation();
399	        }
400	        pass += 1;
401	        println!(
402	            "C1 PASS  {total} length-3 def-use chains, {distinct} distinct opcode signatures \
403	             (recurrence holds); top-signature revise+Stamp e={e_value:.3} (revised={revised} \
404	             choice_dropped={dropped}, mod-64 ceiling per the shipped E4 mechanism)"
405	        );
406	    }
407	
408	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
409	    // C2 NOT-THE-WINDOW — chain signatures vs adjacent-window trigrams, as sets.
410	    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
411	    let window_counts = window_trigram_counts(&eps);
412	    {
413	        let chain_sig_set: HashSet<_> = chain_counts.keys().cloned().collect();
414	        let window_sig_set: HashSet<_> = window_counts.keys().cloned().collect();
415	        let inter = chain_sig_set.intersection(&window_sig_set).count();
416	        let sym_diff = chain_sig_set.symmetric_difference(&window_sig_set).count();
417	        assert!(
418	            sym_diff > 0,
419	            "C2 can-fire: the chain-signature set must differ from the window-trigram set"
420	        );
421	        assert!(
422	            inter > 0,
423	            "C2 can-stay-silent: the two carriers must still share some signatures"
424	        );
425	
426	        let mut top_chain: Vec<_> = chain_counts.iter().collect();
427	        top_chain.sort_by_key(|(_, n)| std::cmp::Reverse(*n));
428	        let mut top_window: Vec<_> = window_counts.iter().collect();
429	        top_window.sort_by_key(|(_, n)| std::cmp::Reverse(*n));
430	
431	        pass += 1;
432	        println!(
433	            "C2 PASS  chain sigs={} window sigs={} intersection={} symmetric_diff={}",
434	            chain_sig_set.len(),
435	            window_sig_set.len(),
436	            inter,
437	            sym_diff
438	        );
439	        println!(
440	            "         top chain sigs:  {:?}",
